[node name="WaterBrushNote" type="Label3D" parent="Notes" unique_id=1689881667]
transform = Transform3D(-0.9080689, 0, -0.41882104, 0, 1, 0, 0.41882104, 0, -0.9080689, 2.0907378, -1.4268028, 8.529017)
pixel_size = 0.002
offset = Vector2(-500, 0)
billboard = 1
double_sided = false
texture_filter = 2
text = "TRIGGER VOLUME

One brush with Collision → Type set to Trigger Volume, which builds an Area3D in the shape of the solid.

Its faces carry a water shader material, Transparency is 0.5, and Occluder is off. Collision Layer 9 is what fps_player.gd samples to decide the player is swimming.

The script is tests/water_volume.gd (extends Brush): it floats RigidBody3D props by density, and reads the waterline from the brush's own top corners."
horizontal_alignment = 0
autowrap_mode = 3
autowrap_trim_flags = 64
width = 1000.0

[node name="CrateNote" type="Label3D" parent="Notes" unique_id=1416060847]
transform = Transform3D(-0.9080689, 0, -0.41882104, 0, 1, 0, 0.41882104, 0, -0.9080689, -4.6997595, 0.012339115, 3.5627654)
pixel_size = 0.002
offset = Vector2(-500, 0)
billboard = 1
double_sided = false
texture_filter = 2
text = "GROUP + RIGID BODY

Seventeen brushes grouped into one node: the slats, the corner posts, the diagonal braces. A group is one node with one merged mesh, and it collides as one convex shape per member, so this crate carries seventeen shapes on its RigidBody3D.

Collision → Type is Rigid Body. The metadata entry density = 400 is what tests/water_volume.gd reads to decide it floats.

For a prop this detailed an imported model would be cheaper and easier to build. This one is here to show brushes can do it, not to say they should."
horizontal_alignment = 0
autowrap_mode = 3
autowrap_trim_flags = 64
width = 1000.0

[node name="StairsNote" type="Label3D" parent="Notes" unique_id=1985708753]
transform = Transform3D(-0.9080689, 0, -0.41882104, 0, 1, 0, 0.41882104, 0, -0.9080689, -8.444707, 0.83928055, 3.9430175)
pixel_size = 0.002
offset = Vector2(-500, 0)
billboard = 1
double_sided = false
texture_filter = 2
text = "COLLISION SPLIT IN TWO

The visible stairs are eight brushes in one group with Collision → Type set to None: they render and nothing else.

Inside them sits a separate brush, StairsCollider — a five-sided wedge left on Static. The player walks up that ramp instead of eight steps.

Every face of the wedge is Empty, and untextured faces are dropped from the mesh in the running game, so it draws nothing there. Transparency 0.8 only changes how it looks in the editor."
horizontal_alignment = 0
autowrap_mode = 3
autowrap_trim_flags = 64
width = 1000.0

[node name="DoorNote" type="Label3D" parent="Notes" unique_id=1525867061]
transform = Transform3D(-0.9080689, 0, -0.41882104, 0, 1, 0, 0.41882104, 0, -0.9080689, 2.8094397, 3.1625319, -2.6337852)
pixel_size = 0.002
offset = Vector2(-500, 0)
billboard = 1
double_sided = false
texture_filter = 2
text = "EMISSION + A LIGHT NODE

One face of this group wears a full material rather than a texture: tests/materials/door_wood_window_lit.tres, dropped onto that face from the FileSystem dock. It is a StandardMaterial3D whose emission texture masks the window panes, so only the glass glows.

Emission makes a surface bright, it does not light anything around it. The glow on the wall and the ground is an AreaLight3D — orange, energy 4, range 10, shadows on.

That light is a child of the group, so moving the door moves the light with it."
horizontal_alignment = 0
autowrap_mode = 3
autowrap_trim_flags = 64
width = 1000.0

[node name="FishNote" type="Label3D" parent="Notes" unique_id=1367540014]
transform = Transform3D(-0.9080689, 0, -0.41882104, 0, 1, 0, 0.41882104, 0, -0.9080689, 2.1184034, -3.989205, 7.4169397)
pixel_size = 0.002
offset = Vector2(-500, 0)
billboard = 1
double_sided = false
texture_filter = 2
text = "SO LONG, AND THANKS FOR ALL THE FISH

Models and brushes share a scene perfectly happily. Also the pool looked empty."
horizontal_alignment = 0
autowrap_mode = 3
autowrap_trim_flags = 64
width = 1000.0

